WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:04.520
There will be a few theorems in this
section

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.070
and let's prove the first one, the
Breakpoint Theorem.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.770
First, let's define the notions of

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:15.020
adjacencies and breakpoints looking at
this permutation.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.245
+3 and +4 are arranged in the
right order.

00:00:18.245 --> 00:00:22.740
In the same order they are arranged in the
identity permutation.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.130
Let's call it, an adjustment.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.520
+4 and +5 is the same story.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.790
+5 and -12 is definitely out of
order.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.244
And certainly, there should be a
breakpoint that cuts between them.

00:00:39.244 --> 00:00:44.032
So, there should be a reversal with one of
its endpoints between +5 and -12

00:00:44.032 --> 00:00:47.224
because otherwise, we will never be

00:00:47.224 --> 00:00:51.610
able to transform this permutation into
that identity permutation.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:53.950
-12, -8, also breakpoint.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:59.130
This is an interesting case:
-8 and -7.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.400
It's not +7 and +8 as we want to

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.270
see in the identity permutation, but maybe
there will be a reversal

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:12.550
that contains both -8 and -7 and
then after reversing,

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.840
they will turn into a perfect permutation
and will correspond to the case

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.340
+7 and +8 and that's why we say
-8 and -7 are in order,

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:27.166
despite being inverted.
-7, -6... the same story.

00:01:27.166 --> 00:01:34.200
-6 +1 is definitely a breakpoint,
adjacency breakpoint.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.225
This is also a breakpoint, think about why
it is a breakpoint.

00:01:37.225 --> 00:01:42.030
Again, breakpoint, breakpoint and finally,
adjacency.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:47.384
We're done not quite yet, because there
are two more implicit elements of this

00:01:47.384 --> 00:01:51.260
permutation, 0 and n+1 that

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.632
describe the start and end of this
permutation.

00:01:53.632 --> 00:01:58.925
And 0, +3 is the break point because

00:01:58.925 --> 00:02:04.010
0 should not be next to +8,
it should be next to +1.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:07.168
And +14 and +15 is adjacency.

00:02:07.168 --> 00:02:11.170
So after we define adjacencies and

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.800
breakpoints, clearly the number of adjacencies
plus the number

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:21.576
of breakpoints in the permutation equals
to the length of the permutation plus 1.

00:02:21.576 --> 00:02:26.020
And before we go further, I want to ask
you a question.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.576
Well, what is the number of breakpoints in

00:02:28.576 --> 00:02:31.980
the identity permutation,
+1, +2, ..., +n?

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:38.640
Of course, this permutation has no
breakpoints, zero, which means

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.470
that sorting by reversal is essentially
breakpoint elimination.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.240
Let's see how it works.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.430
So let's start from the initial
permutation that has six breakpoints.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.770
We move next, there are only four
breakpoints left.

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:58.090
We move left, again, four breakpoints
left.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.860
We move further, two breakpoints, and
finally, in the identity

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:07.920
permutation, zero breakpoints,which means
that every sorting by

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.924
reversal should reduce the number of
breakpoints from six to

00:03:12.924 --> 00:03:17.910
zero in this case.
We don't know what sequence of reversal is

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.810
minimal, but we know that any or each of
them will

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.800
result in the reduction of the number of
breakpoints to zero.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:33.270
Next question: how many breakpoints can be
eliminated by a single reversal?

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:41.280
Well, the answer to this question is
simple; reversal has two ends and nothing

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:47.100
happens with these breakpoints on the left
and on the right of those end points.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.830
Also, every breakpoint that

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:52.930
existed within this interval will remain
the breakpoint.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:57.160
And therefore, the only breakpoints that
can be affected by

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.080
reversals are the two breakpoints on the
end of the reversals.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:08.260
And since at every step, the number of
breakpoints can be reduced by at most,

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:14.266
two, we have the breakpoint theorem.
Reversal distance should be larger or

00:04:14.266 --> 00:04:18.205
equal than the number of breakpoints
over/divided by two.

00:04:18.205 --> 00:04:22.391
And it also immediately suggests a new
Greedy algorithm

00:04:22.391 --> 00:04:27.309
and probably a better Greedy algorithm for
sorting by reversal.

00:04:27.309 --> 00:04:31.485
Indeed, the only thing we need to do to
sort a permutation

00:04:31.485 --> 00:04:36.650
optimally is to reduce the number of
breakpoints by two at every step.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.380
Let's look at our segments

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:45.180
of sorting by reversals and we see that at
this step, we did something silly.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.050
We were supposed to be reducing the number
of breakpoints by two

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.470
at every step, but the number of
breakpoints

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:57.750
actually has not even reduced at this
reversal.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:01.230
Maybe we should have chosen another
reversal that would reduce

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:04.490
the number of breakpoints by two, but you
can check that

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.650
there is no such reversal.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:09.460
And in fact, there are permutations for

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.590
which there are no reversal reducing
number of

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:15.470
breakpoints even by one, which means
whatever you

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.703
do, the number of breakpoints remains the
same.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:23.495
And that's why it turns out that the

00:05:23.495 --> 00:05:27.380
algorithm for sorting by reversals is
actually quite complex.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:29.620
Even if I had an hour or two

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:34.230
of time one on one with you, I wouldn't be
able to explain all

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:38.870
intricacies of the polynomial algorithm
for sorting by reversals.

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:44.780
And that's why I will switch from the
problem of sorting by reversal

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:49.594
to a different, seemingly even more
difficult problem of

00:05:49.594 --> 00:05:55.064
finding rearrangements
and optimal sorting by

00:05:55.064 --> 00:05:58.343
of multi-chromosomal genomes.

